text: <|channel|>analysis<|message|>We need to answer: What causes the seasons on Earth? Provide explanation: Earth's axial tilt relative to its orbital plane, causing varying solar insolation at different latitudes over the year. Also mention Earth's orbit elliptical but not main cause. Provide details: Earth's axis is tilted ~23.5°, so as Earth orbits Sun, different hemispheres receive more direct sunlight at different times. Also mention that the seasons are due to tilt, not distance. Provide explanation of how the tilt leads to varying solar angle, day length, etc. Also mention that the seasons are opposite in hemispheres. Provide mention of the tilt being stable due to angular momentum, but precession changes over long times. Provide mention of the elliptical orbit causing slight variation in distance but not main cause. Provide mention of the tilt causing the sun's declination to change. Provide mention of the effect on temperature. Provide mention of the seasons being defined by the solstices and equinoxes. Provide mention of the
